---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import gb from '../data/gedenkbuch.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="The Leibholz brothers"
  subtitle="Schubin in Posen, then Berlin, then Auschwitz and Chełmno"
  lede="Four men of this surname appear in the German Federal Archives' Memorial Book. Three of them were brothers. The fourth was not, and proving that is one of the two corrections this page makes to the family's own tree.">

  <h2>The entries, as the Bundesarchiv has them</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    Bundesarchiv, <em>Gedenkbuch — Opfer der Verfolgung der Juden unter der nationalsozialistischen
    Gewaltherrschaft in Deutschland 1933–1945</em>, online edition, read 14 September 2026.
    Each entry is cited by its stable identifier.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Birthplace</th><th>Living in</th><th>Deported</th><th>Died</th><th>Entry</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {gb.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{r.name}</strong></td>
          <td>{r.born}</td>
          <td>{r.birthplace}</td>
          <td>{r.residence}</td>
          <td>{r.deportation_1}{r.deportation_2 ? '; ' + r.deportation_2 : ''}</td>
          <td>{r.death_date === 'not documented' ? <span class="muted">not documented</span> : r.death_date}{r.death_place && r.death_place !== 'not documented' ? ' — ' + r.death_place : ''}</td>
          <td class="small"><a href={'https://apps.bundesarchiv.de/gedenkbuch/' + r.gedenkbuch_id}>{r.gedenkbuch_id}</a></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Why Jacob lasted until February 1943, in one line of a form
      <span class="tag doc">Documented &middot; 20 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      The 1939 census supplementary card recorded the ancestry of all four of a person&rsquo;s
      grandparents. <strong>Auguste Leibholz n&eacute;e Luwinski&rsquo;s card reads
      &ldquo;Race&rdquo;: NNNN</strong> &mdash; four times <em>nicht-j&uuml;disch</em>. The German state
      classified Kurt&rsquo;s mother as not Jewish, on its own form, in 1939. Her basis of persecution is
      given as <strong>collective</strong>: the term for someone taken as part of a household rather than
      for their own recorded ancestry.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a <strong>Mischehe</strong>, a mixed marriage, and it is the ordinary explanation for the
      thing that has always looked strange about this family &mdash; that Jacob, a Jew from Schubin, was
      still living at Meraner Stra&szlig;e 2 in 1939 and was not seized until
      <strong>26 February 1943</strong>, the day of the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>, when Berlin&rsquo;s Jews in
      mixed marriages were finally taken. <span class="tag inf">The category is on the record. The
      inference from it is not asserted here.</span>
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Her card also names her birthplace: <strong>Sauerbaum, Kreis R&ouml;ssel, East Prussia</strong>
      &mdash; the same village as <strong>Johanna Thimm</strong>, Kurt&rsquo;s great-grandmother, whose
      surname this archive had already been using as its East Prussian control.
      <a href={base + '/east-prussia/'}>The East Prussia page</a> was built on Ermland parish entries;
      here is the village, from a state document.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Correction one: Jacob did not die in 1945</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      The family tree records <em>“1945, KZ Auschwitz”</em>. The Memorial Book records his deportation
      from Berlin to Auschwitz on <strong>26 February 1943</strong>, and — carefully — <strong>no date
      of death at all</strong>, because the Federal Archives publish one only where it is certain.
    </p>
    <p>
      He was 58. The 1945 in the tree is a family estimate that hardened into a fact.
      <strong>Correct the year; keep the place.</strong> <span class="tag doc">Documented</span>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>And the place is now corroborated, 20 September 2026.</strong> The Reichssippenamt
      census card of 17 May 1939 &mdash; Bundesarchiv R 1509, through Mapping the Lives &mdash; carries
      Jacob in the family block of his wife&rsquo;s record as
      <em>&ldquo;&dagger; before 08.05.1945 <strong>in Auschwitz</strong>&rdquo;</em>. The Memorial Book
      leaves the place blank as well as the date. A second source names it, and it is the place the
      family always said. The date is still not documented anywhere.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Correction two: Georg was not their brother</h2>
  <p>
    The tree makes Georg a fourth son of Sally Leibholz and Friederike Schmul. He cannot be. Jacob was
    born at <strong>Schubin in Posen on 8 August 1884</strong>; Georg at <strong>Hammerstein in West
    Prussia on 7 June 1884</strong>. <strong>Sixty-two days apart, one mother.</strong> Both dates
    come from the Bundesarchiv, independently of the family, and that arithmetic is the whole of the
    argument.
  </p>
  <p>
    A Memorial Book search on the surname returns fifty Leibholz entries, and they cluster in two
    places: Schubin in Posen, and Hammerstein, Karwen and B&auml;rwalde in West Prussia and Pomerania.
    <strong>Those two clusters are not two families.</strong> Sally Leibholz &mdash; Jacob&rsquo;s own
    father &mdash; was born at Hammerstein, and the Schubin marriage register says so in as many words:
    on 24 November 1874 <em>der Kaufmann Sally Leibholz, geboren zu Hammerstein, wohnhaft zu
    Hammerstein</em> married Friederica Schmul of Schubin. <strong>It is one family in the two towns
    it lived in</strong> &mdash; the place it came from, and the place it moved to, with a registrar
    recording the move as it happened.
    <span class="tag disp">Corrected 20 September 2026</span>
  </p>
  <p class="small muted">
    The distance was doing work in the argument that it cannot do. <strong>What rules out brotherhood
    is the sixty-two days, not the two hundred kilometres</strong> &mdash; and with the geography gone,
    a Leibholz born at Hammerstein in 1884 is not a stranger sharing a surname but a relative in the
    ancestral town. That makes <em>cousin</em> the strong reading rather than <em>a different family</em>,
    which is what the next panel has said all along.
  </p>
  <div class="panel" id="marriage1874">
    <h3>The marriage entry, read 22 September 2026 <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      Everything below this panel was argued from a family record and the Gedenkbuch.
      <strong>It is now on a document.</strong> Standesamt Schubin, marriage akt nr 9 of
      <strong>24 November 1874</strong>, the ninth and last marriage of that year, gives the groom as
      <em>der Kaufmann Sally Leibholz</em>, j&uuml;discher Religion, <strong>sieben und zwanzig Jahre
      alt</strong>, <strong>geboren zu Hammerstein, wohnhaft zu Hammerstein</strong>. Twenty-seven in
      November 1874 puts his birth in 1847 &mdash; the year this archive has always carried for him,
      and one that no surviving register can confirm, because Koszalin answered on 16 September that
      the Hammerstein books are gone. <strong>The marriage entry did what the destroyed birth register
      would have done.</strong> The bride is <em>Friederica Schmul</em>, born and living at Schubin.
      So the move east is not an inference: it happens at this wedding.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the entry reaches a generation higher than anything else this archive holds. Sally is
      <em>Sohn des Kaufmann <strong>Jacob Leibholz</strong> und der <strong>Wilhelmine geb.
      Pollnow</strong></em> &mdash; Kurt&rsquo;s great-great-grandparents, neither of whom existed here
      in any form before that evening. The grandson born at Schubin ten years later was named
      <strong>Jacob</strong> for him. The maiden name was the least certain thing on the page &mdash; Kurrent, where P, T and Z
      confuse &mdash; so it was <strong>tested against a list of German-Jewish surnames</strong>. In the
      Bundesarchiv Gedenkbuch, <strong>POLLNOW returns eight people; TOLLNOW, ZOLLNOW and SOLLNOW return
      none</strong>, with Leibholz at 50 and a nonsense string at zero as controls. Of the four ways that
      capital could be read, only one is a name anybody bore. That is not proof, but it is no longer a
      guess.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Archiwum Pa&#324;stwowe w Bydgoszczy, USC Szubin Miasto, sygn. 141, akt ma&#322;&#380;e&#324;stwa
      nr 9/1874, scan 10, on genealogiawarchiwach.pl. Found because Bydgoszcz answered a letter by
      pointing at a portal where the registers had been published since 2015.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A likelier reading <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Sally Leibholz was himself born at Hammerstein</strong> &mdash; now documented, see the
      panel above &mdash; on 7 July 1847, and married at Szubin on 24 November 1874. So the family had people in both towns. The cleanest explanation is
      that Georg belonged to the Hammerstein branch Sally came from — a nephew or a cousin of Jacob's,
      not a brother. That keeps every documented date intact and only moves one line on the chart.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Status of Georg in this archive: <span class="tag disp">Disputed</span> — right surname, right
      family, wrong generation, pending a Schubin or Hammerstein register.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The question has been put to the two archives that can answer it <span class="tag doc">15 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      Both towns are now in Poland, and both civil registries began in 1874 — so a birth entry from
      <strong>1884 names both parents outright</strong>, which is precisely what settles whether Georg
      was Jacob's brother or his cousin.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Archive</th><th>Asked about</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Archiwum Państwowe w Bydgoszczy</strong></td><td>Standesamt <strong>Schubin</strong> 1884 — the birth of <strong>Jacob Leibholz</strong>, 8 August 1884</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Archiwum Państwowe w Koszalinie</strong></td><td>Standesamt <strong>Hammerstein</strong> 1884 — the birth of <strong>Georg Leibholz</strong>, 7 June 1884 — and any Jewish community register for <strong>Sally Leibholz</strong>, born there 7 July 1847</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="small">
      Written in Polish, each with an English summary, and each told what the other has been asked. Both
      set out the arithmetic plainly: two children of one mother cannot be born two months apart in two
      provinces. <strong>If both entries survive, the parents' names decide it in a single line.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      A note on method, since it nearly cost a bounce. The Polish state archives do <em>not</em> use the
      <code>ap.gov.pl</code> domain that most references give — the live one is
      <code>archiwa.gov.pl</code>. Checked before sending, as everything now is.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the dates do when you lay them side by side</h2>
  <p>
    Georg went first: Berlin to the Łódź ghetto on <strong>1 November 1941</strong>, then to Chełmno on
    <strong>13 May 1942</strong>. His daughter <strong>Elli</strong>, born in Berlin on 7 November 1915
    and working as a secretary, is recorded in the family's tree as dying <em>“after 13 May 1942”</em> —
    which is his transport date exactly. She went with him.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the Berlin round-up. Jacob was deported on <strong>26 February 1943</strong>, the day before
    the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>. <strong>Gertrud Leibke</strong>, born in Berlin on 13 July 1894, died in
    the city itself on <strong>27 February 1943</strong> — the day of it. Six weeks later, on
    <strong>10 April 1943</strong>, Samuel was dead at Auschwitz.
  </p>
  <p>
    Oskar lasted longest. Berlin to Theresienstadt on <strong>17 March 1943</strong>, and Theresienstadt
    to Auschwitz on <strong>16 October 1944</strong>, where the Memorial Book gives that same day as the
    date of his death.
  </p>

  <h2>The addresses, and two corrections</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    <em>Mapping the Lives</em> (Tracing the Past e.V.), built on the <strong>census of 17 May
    1939</strong>, read 14 September 2026. Every district it gives matches the Gedenkbuch
    independently — and it adds the street.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th><th>Address</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Jacob Leibholz</strong></td><td>08.08.1884</td><td>before 08.05.1945</td><td><strong>Meraner Str. 2, Schöneberg</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Auguste Leibholz née Luwinski</strong></td><td>28.08.1893</td><td>—</td><td><strong>Meraner Str. 2, Schöneberg</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Siegfried Leibholz</strong></td><td>05.08.1925</td><td>—</td><td><strong>Meraner Str. 2, Schöneberg</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Samuel Leibholz</td><td>28.06.1877</td><td>10.04.1943</td><td>Kommandantenstr. 29, Kreuzberg</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Oskar Leibholz</td><td>13.05.1887</td><td>16.10.1944</td><td><strong>Waghäuseler Str. 9/10, Wilmersdorf</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gertrud Leibholz née Leibke</td><td>13.07.1894</td><td>27.02.1943</td><td><strong>Waghäuseler Str. 9/10, Wilmersdorf</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Georg Leibholz</td><td>07.06.1884</td><td>13.05.1942</td><td>Michaelkirchstr. 26, Mitte</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lydia Leibholz née Perl</td><td>16.01.1885</td><td>13.05.1942</td><td>Michaelkirchstr. 26, Mitte</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Gertrud was Oskar's wife, not Georg's <span class="tag doc">Settled</span></h3>
    <p>
      The family tree attaches Gertrud Leibke to Georg, and duplicates her onto Oskar. The census
      settles it: <strong>Gertrud and Oskar share an address</strong> — Waghäuseler Str. 9/10,
      Wilmersdorf, which is exactly the district the Bundesarchiv gives for Oskar. Georg was in Mitte,
      with someone else.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Jacob was not taken from the family flat <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      The table above puts Jacob, Auguste and Siegfried together at <strong>Meraner Straße 2</strong>
      on census night, 17 May 1939. On 20 September 2026 the Arolsen Archives served the transport
      list itself, and it gives a different street.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Wave 44 &mdash; 30th Osttransport to CC Auschwitz, 26 February 1943</em>, page 153,
      line <strong>1039</strong>: &ldquo;<em>Leibholz &middot; Jakob Israel &middot; 8.8.84 &middot;
      Schubin &middot; Beruf <strong>ohne</strong> &middot; verh. ja &middot; Alter 58 &middot;
      arbeitsfähig ja &middot; Wohnung <strong>Treuchtlingerstr. 4</strong> &middot; Kennkarte
      A492552 &middot; Kennzeichen 31822</em>&rdquo;. An index card of the Berlin AJDC office
      repeats the address and adds one word: <strong>&ldquo;b/Spier&rdquo;</strong> &mdash;
      <em>bei</em> Spier, care of a family called Spier.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same page of the list carries &ldquo;49 b/Grindel&rdquo;, &ldquo;b/Gottfeld&rdquo; and
      &ldquo;b/Neumann&rdquo; against other names. <strong>He was lodging in somebody else&rsquo;s
      flat</strong>, as many of the 1,100 on that list were. Between the census and the transport
      he had been moved out of his own home, and nothing read here says when, or by whom.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      One column is worth reading twice. In the 1938 directory he is <em>Jaques, Kaufmann</em>, with
      a clothing firm at Krausenstraße 59&ndash;60. On the list of February 1943 the occupation
      column says <strong>ohne</strong>. None.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the Spiers have names <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      The 1939 census database was then searched by <em>street and house number</em> rather than by
      surname. <strong>Treuchtlinger Straße 4 held seventeen persecuted people from eleven family
      names</strong> on census night &mdash; Brück, Cohn, Eisenberg, Eisenberger, Kaphan,
      Kochanowska, Löwenheim, Podbielski, Rosenstock, Sundheimer &mdash; and Spier.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>SPIER, MAX</strong>, born 12 April 1864, died 26 March 1941.
      <strong>SPIER, HANS</strong>, born 2 December 1894.
      <strong>SPIER, ALICE née WOLLSTEIN</strong>, born 3 November 1899. The last two both died
      before 8 May 1945. The two letters on Jacob&rsquo;s card are a household with three names in
      it, and by the time he was lodging there the eldest was already dead.
    </p>
    <p>
      Eleven surnames at one address in May 1939 is not an ordinary Berlin apartment block. Jacob
      was moved into a house that was <em>already full</em>, three and a half years before the
      transport.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And the directory gives the house, floor by floor <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      Every earlier pass at the Berlin address books read <em>Teil I</em>, householders by name.
      There is a <strong>Teil IV</strong> that lists the same people <em>by street</em>, house by
      house, owner first and tenants under him. The 1939 volume, printed page 1629:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p class="small">
        <strong>Treuchtlinger Str.</strong> (Postamt Berlin W 30) &middot; <em>Rechte Seite</em><br />
        2 geh. z. Barbarossastr. 36a<br />
        <strong>4 E Eisenberg, G., vw. Handelsgerichtsrat</strong> &middot; Bayer, W., Oberstleutn.
        &middot; Eberhardt, C., Kfm. &middot; Jakob, W., Maurer &middot; Mannowsky, S., vw. Geh.
        Reg. Rat &middot; Petras, M., kfm. Angest. &middot; Rosenstock, L., kfm. Angest. &middot;
        <strong>Spier, H., Handelsvertr.</strong> &middot; <strong>&mdash; M., Vertret.</strong>
        &middot; Sundheimer, M., Handelsvertr. &middot; Wendorf, R., Abteilungsleit.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The <strong>E</strong> is <em>Eigentümer</em>. The building belonged to
      <strong>Gertrud Eisenberg née Haake</strong>, born 9 June 1879, a commercial court
      councillor&rsquo;s widow &mdash; and the census has her living in it.
      <strong>Spier, H.</strong> is <strong>Hans Spier</strong>, born 2 December 1894, a commercial
      agent; the dash line under him is a second Spier, <strong>M.</strong>, who is
      <strong>Max</strong>, born 12 April 1864 and dead by March 1941.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      That dash is the convention that cost this archive four Leibholz households in a single day
      last week. Only the first name of a run is spelt out. Read carelessly, Hans Spier lives at
      Treuchtlinger Straße 4 alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Who was still called Leibholz in Berlin afterwards <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      The pre-war volumes track this surname disappearing &mdash; seventeen householders in 1938,
      six in 1942, every name carrying <em>Israel</em> gone. The opposite question had never been
      asked. The post-war books run to 1980 and are set in Antiqua, so the text layer is clean enough
      to read: <strong>Leibholz returns nineteen pages across seventeen volumes</strong>, and six
      names across thirty years.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>These volumes are not searched by name.</strong> The OCR prints this surname
      <em>Leiphotz</em>, <em>Lelpholz</em>, <em>Leipkslz</em> and <em>Leibbolz</em> &mdash; five of the eleven
      readable telephone volumes mangle it, and a search for the correct spelling misses every one, while a
      common-name control (Schmidt, 133&ndash;191 hits a volume) looks healthy throughout.
      <strong>A common-name control cannot clear a rare-name negative.</strong> What works is the alphabet:
      the name falls in a known slot, so the whole slot is printed and read, and an absence is recorded only
      when a name above it and a name below it are both in hand. Everything below is read that way.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Agnes</strong> is the one that closes a loop. &ldquo;<em>Leibholz Agnes, Rentn., SW 61
      Mehringdamm 109</em>&rdquo; in 1957 and 1959 &mdash; and still at
      <strong>Mehringdamm 109</strong> &mdash; read at the slot, she is in <strong>eight consecutive
      telephone books, 1972&ndash;73 to 1979&ndash;80</strong>, on one number, and the slot is complete and
      empty in 1980&ndash;81. <strong>Twenty-three years at one address</strong>, 1957 to 1979&ndash;80.
      Two of those eight volumes print her <em>Leihholz</em> and <em>Leibheiz</em>. Heinrich Leibholz is this archive&rsquo;s control case, the man who
      proved that vanishing from the directory after 1942 does not mean death, because he died on
      9 August 1948; the census gives his wife as <strong>Agnes née Kerger</strong>, born 1889. And
      <strong>Sophie Leibholz, writing from Shanghai in 1946</strong>, named him with
      &ldquo;wife Agnes&rdquo; and asked where he was. He had survived, he died two years later,
      and his widow lived on at Mehringdamm for another quarter-century.
    </p>
    <p>
      The others: <strong>Ella</strong>, a <em>Krankenschwester</em> at Dominicusstraße 43 and then
      Willmanndamm 3, 1957 to 1970 &mdash; and a nurse of that name is in the 1938 directory in the
      same district. <strong>Charlotte</strong> at Hasenheide. <strong>Gerhard</strong>,
      <em>Dr., Dipl.-Volkswirt</em>, in Steglitz &mdash; <em>not</em> the constitutional lawyer of
      the same name, who died at Göttingen. <strong>Felix</strong> at Wiesbadener Straße 57, and
      <strong>Hilde</strong> at the same flat six years later &mdash; and the slot read puts Hilde in
      <strong>nine consecutive volumes, 1972&ndash;73 to 1980&ndash;81</strong>, which makes her
      <strong>the last Leibholz in the post-war Berlin books</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>Erna of Streitstraße 39 in Spandau is not one of them.</strong> She is a
      <strong>LEIPHOLZ</strong>, and the alphabet around her is what settles it: that line sits between
      <em>Leipert</em> and <em>Leipner</em>, where Leipholz belongs and Leibholz cannot, and the
      1975&ndash;76 through 1979&ndash;80 volumes print the P plainly, on one number throughout. She and
      Hilde are in the same 1980&ndash;81 book, three places apart in the alphabet and under different
      spellings of what the directory itself called one name.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Not one is documented as belonging to the Schubin family, and that is the point. <strong>The
      survivors of this surname in Berlin are the other Leibholz families.</strong> Kurt&rsquo;s
      people were in Johannesburg, in Potsdam, and in a grave at Auschwitz. &mdash; And one absence
      was looked for on purpose: <strong>Frida Leibbholz née Spree</strong>, Samuel&rsquo;s widow,
      is in none of the forty volumes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The eighth spelling, and what it has produced <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      On 20 September the Berliner Adressbuch was found to print its own cross-reference &mdash;
      <strong>&ldquo;Leipholz, s. a. Leibholz&rdquo;</strong>, and in the 1940 volume the other way round.
      The compiler of the city these people lived in treated the two names as one for at least twenty-seven
      years. <strong>Every bounded negative this archive had recorded was one spelling short of complete.</strong>
      This is what the eighth spelling has produced since.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Friedrich Leipholz</strong> is the fullest life of them. An <em>Angestellter</em> at Berliner
      Straße 5a in Tegel in 1957 and 1959; a <em>Pensionär</em> at <strong>Schwabstraße 4 in
      Reinickendorf</strong> from 1961, with the same neighbours in the street section seven years apart; and
      in the telephone book at that flat in <strong>1970&ndash;71, 1972&ndash;73 and 1973&ndash;74</strong>.
      He is <strong>absent from the 1974&ndash;75 volume and every one after it</strong>, on a slot that is
      complete and empty. A man who retires between 1959 and 1961 was born around 1895, which puts him in
      Jacob Leibholz&rsquo;s generation.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And his own borough has now been read out.</strong> All twenty-one years of Reinickendorf&rsquo;s
      death-register indexes, <strong>1974 to 1994</strong>, bracketed on both sides of both spellings:
      <strong>his death is not registered there.</strong> So he moved and died elsewhere, or he died after
      1994 at nearly a hundred, or he simply gave up the telephone. Heinrich Leibholz &mdash; who left the
      Berlin directory in 1942 and did not die until 1948 &mdash; forbids the easy reading.
      <strong>Leaving a telephone book is not dying.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      The twenty-one years turned up exactly one Leipholz, and she is new: <strong>Marie</strong>, dying in
      the borough Friedrich lived in.
    </p>
    <figure class="doc-scan">
      <img src={base + '/leipholz-marie-1988.jpg'} alt="A typewritten row from the alphabetical index to the Reinickendorf death register for 1988, reading: Leipholz, Marie" />
      <figcaption class="small muted">
        <strong>Namensverzeichnis zum Sterberegister, Standesamt Reinickendorf 1988</strong>, P Rep. 130
        Nr. 1484, page 76, printed between <em>Leinkram</em> and <em>Leisegang</em>. The full row reads
        <strong>&ldquo;Leipholz | Marie | 05.11. | 2306 | ev.&rdquo;</strong> Read at the image on
        23 September 2026 through pdf.js over HTTP range requests; nothing downloaded.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The text layer rendered her death date <em>@507</em> and was not legible enough to date her at all.
      <strong>At the image it is the fifth of November</strong> &mdash; which is why a layer that cannot be
      read is said to be unreadable rather than rounded into a fact.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the image carries a column the text layer never showed at all: <strong>she is
      &ldquo;ev.&rdquo; &mdash; evangelisch, Protestant.</strong> That column reads <em>mosaisch</em> for the
      Jewish woman in the Spandau 1982 index, and <em>mosaisch</em> for Leinkram Gertrud three lines above
      Marie on her own page, so the distinction is being made on that very sheet.
      <strong>A Protestant Leipholz belongs with the Masurian Leipholz family of Pianki and Arys</strong>,
      whom this archive already holds and already calls certainly not the same people &mdash; not with Jacob
      Leibholz of Schubin. It is one word and it proves nothing. For once it points <em>away</em> from this
      family rather than toward it, which is worth as much.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The rest of the cohort: <strong>Klara</strong>, a <em>Putzmacherin</em> &mdash; a milliner &mdash; at
      Dieffenbachstraße 58 in 1957 and 1959, the same street and house number where a <strong>Paul
      Leipholz, Schlosser</strong> was listed in 1924. <strong>Erna</strong> at Streitstraße 39 in Spandau,
      in six consecutive telephone books on one number, 1975&ndash;76 to 1980&ndash;81 &mdash; and
      <em>not</em> the Erna Frida Else Leipholz who died at Schöneberg on 13 January 1975, because a woman
      who died in January 1975 is not listed on her own line for the next six years. <strong>They are two
      different women of the same name.</strong> And <strong>Elisabeth Weiler née Leipholz</strong>, born
      1891 at Neustettin in Pomerania, who is in the Bundesarchiv&rsquo;s residents&rsquo; list and not in the
      Gedenkbuch, and now it is clear why: <strong>she got out, to Palestine.</strong>
      <strong>Not one of them is joined to Jacob Leibholz of Schubin by any document held here.</strong>
      The directory&rsquo;s own cross-reference is why they are worth holding, and it is not a pedigree.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Samuel had a wife, and she was not Jewish either <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      The same search, run on <strong>Kommandantenstraße 29</strong> &mdash; Samuel the hat
      wholesaler&rsquo;s address &mdash; returns two people, on consecutive census cards.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Born</th><th>&ldquo;Race&rdquo;</th><th>Died</th><th>Card</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Samuel Leibholz</strong></td><td>28.06.1877, <strong>Szubin (Schubin)</strong></td><td><strong>JJJJ</strong></td><td>10.04.1943, Auschwitz</td><td>VZ153419</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Frida Leibbholz née Spree</strong></td><td>12.05.1888, <strong>Berlin</strong></td><td><strong>NNNN</strong></td><td>—</td><td>VZ153420</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>So both brothers were in mixed marriages.</strong> Jacob married Auguste née Luwinski,
      NNNN, persecuted &ldquo;collectively&rdquo;. Samuel married Frida née Spree, NNNN. Two men
      from Schubin, two non-Jewish German wives &mdash; and <strong>both men were deported
      anyway</strong>, Jacob on 26 February 1943 and Samuel to Auschwitz, where he died on 10 April.
      Neither wife has a death date in the database. A <em>Mischehe</em> delayed the two brothers
      and it did not save them.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Samuel is <strong>not in the Arolsen index at all</strong>, searched twice, the second time
      with the consent declaration ticked. He is in the German Federal Archives census set with a
      card number, a household and a wife. The gap is Arolsen&rsquo;s, not this family&rsquo;s.
      The clerk spelt her surname <strong>Leibbholz</strong>, with a doubled B.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And Oskar&rsquo;s own registration card says so too <span class="tag doc">Corroborated</span></h3>
    <p>
      The paragraph above was argued from a shared address in the 1939 census. On 20 September 2026
      the Arolsen Archives served <strong>Oskar&rsquo;s Berlin registration card</strong> &mdash; a
      <em>Vordruck Nr.&nbsp;25&nbsp;B</em> in the series <em>Berlin: Registration Cards from Jewish
      Residents</em> &mdash; and it has two lines on it: &ldquo;<strong>Oskar Israel, verh.,
      13.5.87, Schubin</strong>&rdquo; and, directly beneath, &ldquo;<strong>Ehefr. geb. Leibke
      &hellip; Gertrud Sara, verh., 13.7.94, Berlin</strong>&rdquo;. Their <em>Kennkarte</em> numbers
      are consecutive: <strong>A&nbsp;442&nbsp;462</strong> and <strong>A&nbsp;442&nbsp;461</strong>.
      Their tracing-case numbers are consecutive too &mdash; T/D 52.971 and T/D 52.972. The census
      gave an address; the card gives a marriage.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Oskar&rsquo;s two transports, from the two ends <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      The Memorial Book gives Oskar two dates. Four Arolsen documents now give the paperwork behind
      them, and it fits together.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Gestapo list for the <strong>&ldquo;4th Large Transport of the Elderly&rdquo; of 17 March
      1943</strong> names its own transport <strong>I/90</strong> and carries, at page 161 line 54,
      &ldquo;<em>Leibholz &middot; Oskar Israel &middot; 13.5.87 &middot; Schubin &middot; W.50,
      Marburgerstr. 13 &middot; 014577</em>&rdquo;. An index card of the Berlin AJDC office repeats
      the birthplace as <strong>Schubin/Bromberg</strong>, the address as <strong>Berlin W 50,
      Marburger Straße 13</strong>, and files him as &ldquo;<em>49/30999 II &mdash; 4.gr.Alterstrsp.
      17.3.43</em>&rdquo;.
    </p>
    <p>
      And in Terezín a Czech clerk filled in a card headed <em>Ústřední kartotéka &mdash;
      transporty</em>: &ldquo;<em>Leibholz Oskar &middot; 13.5.1887 &middot;
      <strong>Deportace na východ: Číslo Er 606, dne 16.X.1944</strong> &middot; (původní transportní
      číslo: <strong>11025-I/90</strong>)</em>&rdquo;. The original transport number on the Terezín
      card is the Berlin transport&rsquo;s own designation. <strong>The two ends of the journey name
      each other.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      What the Terezín card does <em>not</em> say is where he was sent. It says
      <em>na východ</em> &mdash; to the east &mdash; and gives a letter, a number and a date. The
      Memorial Book&rsquo;s Auschwitz is not written on it, and is not claimed here as read.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>He travelled alone, and there is a card that says why <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      Oskar&rsquo;s line on the transport list carries no <em>Ehepaar</em> bracket, although the same
      sheet brackets other couples. Gertrud has a document of her own, and it explains it.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is a printed card of the <strong>Kultusvereinigung Berlin</strong> headed
      &ldquo;<em>Zählkarte für <s>auswandernde</s> Personen</em>&rdquo; &mdash; a counting card for
      <em>emigrating</em> persons, with the word <em>emigrating</em> struck through in ink. In the
      columns: <em>Leibholz geb. Leibke &middot; Gertrud &middot; 13.7.94, Berlin &middot;
      verh.</em> And in the column headed <strong>Zielland</strong>, country of destination:
      <strong>&ldquo;verstorben 27/2.43&rdquo;</strong>. At the foot, the household address
      &mdash; <strong>Marburgerstr. 13</strong> &mdash; and the date it was written,
      <strong>8 March 1943</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>She died on 27 February 1943, eighteen days before the transport left.</strong> That
      date was already on this page from the Memorial Book; it is now on a card filled in nine days
      afterwards by the office that knew her, with her address on it. Oskar went to Theresienstadt
      a widower of three weeks.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      An emigration form with <em>emigrating</em> crossed out and a death written into the box for
      the country she was going to. The card does not say how she died, and 27 February 1943 in
      Berlin was the day of the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>. This archive is not writing a cause of death
      out of a coincidence of dates.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Georg's wife was Lydia née Perl, and they died on the same day <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Lydia Leibholz née Perl</strong>, born 16 January 1885, lived at Michaelkirchstr. 26 —
      Georg's address — and <strong>died on 13 May 1942</strong>, the exact date of Georg's transport
      from the Łódź ghetto to Chełmno. <strong>They went together.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      And <strong>two Pages of Testimony</strong> filed for Georg at Yad Vashem, by
      <strong>two different nieces</strong> and years apart, both give his wife's name as
      <strong>Lydia</strong>. Neither mentions Gertrud. Three sources, none quoting the other.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The two nieces disagree about where he died — one wrote Riga, the other Auschwitz — and the
      Bundesarchiv, working from the transport lists, says Łódź and then Chełmno. Two families
      remembered two different wrong places, which is what happens when nobody is left who saw it.
      It does not make them unreliable about the rest: they agreed on his wife, his city and his trade.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And Meraner Straße 2 held fourteen of them <span class="tag inf">Inferred, strongly</span></h3>
    <p>
      Searched by street, that one address returns <strong>fourteen people in the persecution
      records, from six unrelated families</strong> — the three Leibholzes, three Salingers and a
      Peyser, two Schlesingers, an Ida Weil, an Anna Puff, and the Schades: Friedrich, Klara, and
      <strong>a boy called Heinz born in July 1933</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Six families crowded into one building, all appearing in the May 1939 census, is the signature
      of a <strong><em>Judenhaus</em></strong> — a house Jews were moved into after being evicted
      from their own homes under the tenancy law of 30 April 1939. The borough housing files would
      prove it; this is the shape of it.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      If so, <strong>the Leibholzes were not living in their own flat by 1939</strong>. They had been
      concentrated there with five other families — and it is from that building that Jacob was
      taken on 26 February 1943.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Auguste is in the persecution records herself</h3>
    <p>
      She was not Jewish, and she is in the dataset anyway — <strong>Auguste Leibholz née Luwinski,
      Meraner Straße 2, Schöneberg</strong> — registered at her Jewish husband's address in the census
      whose supplementary card asked about grandparents. That is what a <em>Mischehe</em> looked like
      on paper in May 1939. Note the name: <strong>she is recorded as Frau Leibholz</strong>, which
      the family tree does not carry.
    </p>
    <p>
      And she is on the Meraner Straße list. A German woman in a <em>Mischehe</em> did not have to
      follow her husband into such a building. Some did. It is the clearest thing this archive has
      about what she chose — and she got the younger boy out to Sommerfeld in January 1943, the month
      before his father was taken.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Kurt is absent.</strong> A search on Luwinski across the whole database returns two
      people: Auguste under her married name, and an unrelated man from Königsberg.
      <strong>The gap in the Berlin record is the emigration.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What is still open about Gertrud <span class="tag inf">Open</span></h3>
    <p>
      Which brother she married is now answered. What is not: <strong>she died in Berlin on 27
      February 1943</strong>, the day of the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>, and not in a camp. Yad Vashem
      categorises her as <strong>murdered</strong>. A death in Berlin on that particular day has an
      obvious and terrible range of explanations, and the record does not choose between them.
      The Berlin death register would.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>There is no stone for him <span class="tag doc">Checked</span></h3>
    <p>
      Against the register of all <strong>738 Stolpersteine in Berlin-Schöneberg</strong> and the
      citywide database: <strong>none at Meraner Straße, and none for any Leibholz in the district.</strong>
      The Leibholz stones that do exist in Berlin belong to other Leibholz families.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Until today nobody knew the address to put one at. An application is drafted, in German, with
      the Gedenkbuch entry, the transport, the 1939 household and the two addressees — the
      Koordinierungsstelle and the Tempelhof-Schöneberg borough office.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The great-grandparents, documented at last</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      The chart on the front page of this site has said <strong>&ldquo;Sally Leibholz &amp; Friederike
      Schmul&rdquo;</strong> since the first day — <strong>on the family tree&rsquo;s word and nothing
      else</strong>. It has a source now, and of all the places it could have come from it came from
      this one.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p><strong>SAMUEL ISRAEL LEIBHOLZ</strong> — born <strong>28 June 1877</strong> at Schubin<br />
      <strong>Father&rsquo;s first name: SALI</strong><br />
      <strong>Mother&rsquo;s first name: FRIDERICKE</strong><br />
      <strong>Mother&rsquo;s maiden name: SCHMUL</strong><br />
      Married — spouse <strong>Frieda</strong><br />
      Died <strong>Auschwitz, 10 April 1943</strong></p>
      <p class="small">Yad Vashem 5415605. Content note: <em>document number 18258/1943</em>.
      Source: <strong>the Auschwitz Death Registers</strong>, State Museum Auschwitz-Birkenau.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>Both great-grandparents are named on a German death register kept at Auschwitz, against a
      numbered entry, for a man born at Schubin in 1877.</strong> The spellings are <em>Sali</em> and
      <em>Fridericke</em> — what the clerk wrote. It is the first primary source this archive has ever
      held for that generation. And it gives Samuel a wife, <strong>Frieda</strong>, whom nobody here
      knew about.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>A test was built out of it and run.</strong> Yad Vashem can be searched on mother&rsquo;s
      maiden name. SCHMUL returns people from Berlin to Łódź to Komarów — it is a common name — and
      <strong>exactly one Leibholz</strong>. No brother of Jacob and Samuel is recorded anywhere in that
      database with those parents. <strong>That is a limit of the records, not of the family:</strong>
      Jacob&rsquo;s and Oskar&rsquo;s entries come from <em>deportation lists</em>, which carry no
      parents&rsquo; fields at all. Only a Page of Testimony or a death register carries parents, and of
      the four brothers only Samuel has one. It also settles the shape of the Georg question — he will
      never be included or excluded by a parents&rsquo; field, because his records do not have one
      either. <strong>His niece remains the route.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Two people survived to write these names down</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      A <strong>Page of Testimony</strong> is not a list. It is a form filled in <em>by a person who
      knew the dead</em>, and it names that person and what they were to the victim. Eighteen exist
      under this surname. <strong>Two have been opened.</strong>
    </p>

    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>The page</th><th>What it records</th><th>Who wrote it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Elli Struzik née Leibholz</strong><br /><span class="small muted">YV 13689593 · item 1819733</span></td>
          <td class="small">Born <strong>7 November 1915</strong> at Berlin — matching this archive&rsquo;s register to the day. <strong>Father: Georg. Mother: Lydia. Mother&rsquo;s maiden name: PERL.</strong> Married to Harry. A <strong>secretary</strong>. Twenty-six. Deported Berlin to the <strong>Łódź ghetto, 1 November 1941</strong>, and Łódź to <strong>Chełmno, 13 May 1942</strong>.</td>
          <td><strong>MARGOT COX</strong><br /><span class="small">Cousin</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Georg Laibholz</strong><br /><span class="small muted">item 1684365</span></td>
          <td class="small">Born <strong>1883</strong>. Married <strong>Lidia née Peretz</strong>. Of Berlin. Profession: <strong>factory owner</strong>.</td>
          <td><strong>IRMGARD SHTERN</strong><br /><span class="small">Niece</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <p class="verdict disp">
      <strong>These are not Kurt&rsquo;s relatives on any evidence this archive holds.</strong> The
      position argued above is that <strong>Georg is not Jacob&rsquo;s brother</strong> — Jacob born at
      Schubin in Posen on 8 August 1884, Georg at Hammerstein in West Prussia two months earlier, and
      two men cannot be brothers on those dates. Margot Cox and Irmgard Shtern belong to
      <em>Georg&rsquo;s</em> line.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And that is exactly why they are worth everything.</strong>
      <strong>Irmgard Shtern is Georg&rsquo;s niece, so her parent was Georg&rsquo;s sibling</strong> —
      and Georg&rsquo;s parents are the single fact that would settle whether his line touches Sally
      Leibholz and Friederike Schmul of Schubin at all. <strong>A niece is one generation from the
      answer to the question this page was built to ask.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Three things that were assertions here are now documented: Georg&rsquo;s wife <strong>was</strong>
      Lydia née Perl, and it is on her daughter&rsquo;s page in a cousin&rsquo;s hand; Elli&rsquo;s death
      is no longer &ldquo;after 13 May 1942&rdquo; but <strong>13 May 1942, at Chełmno</strong>; and
      Georg was a <strong>factory owner</strong>, which nobody here knew.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      And three discrepancies are carried rather than resolved. <strong>Georg&rsquo;s birth year is now
      1884, 1885 and 1883</strong> — three sources, three years. <strong>Lydia&rsquo;s maiden name is
      PERL on one page and PERETZ on the other.</strong> And his place of death is <strong>Auschwitz</strong>
      on this page but <strong>Chełmno</strong> on the deportation lists. A Page of Testimony is written
      from memory, years afterwards, by somebody who was not there; <strong>the deportation list is the
      better source and this archive keeps Chełmno.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The transport has a number</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      This site has carried Jacob&rsquo;s deportation as a date — <strong>26 February 1943, Berlin to
      Auschwitz</strong> — since the first day. Yad Vashem&rsquo;s Central Database of Shoah
      Victims&rsquo; Names, searched properly on 17 September 2026, carries a field this archive never
      had.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p><strong>Details of Transport:</strong> <em>Transport 30 from Berlin, City of Berlin, Germany
      to Auschwitz Birkenau, Extermination Camp, Poland on 26/02/1943.</em></p>
      <p class="small">Yad Vashem record <strong>13520936</strong>, item IDs 11572198 and 4113817.
      Date of birth 08/08/1884; place of birth Schubin; permanent residence Berlin;
      <strong>citizenship Germany</strong>.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>Transport 30.</strong> Not a date any more — a numbered train, with a list, that other
      people were also on.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And it comes from a memorial book this archive did not know it was standing on.</strong>
      That record is sourced not to the Bundesarchiv but to the <em>Gedenkbuch Berlins der jüdischen
      Opfer des Nationalsozialismus</em> — Freie Universität Berlin, Edition Hentrich, Berlin 1995.
      <strong>Berlin&rsquo;s own memorial book, a different work from the national one cited on every
      other page here</strong>, and the source of the transport number. It has not been consulted
      directly, and it should be: for Auguste, for Siegfried, and for an address.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Oskar&rsquo;s record</strong> (13449776) gives the two deportations and closes the
      distance between them: Berlin to <strong>Theresienstadt on 17 March 1943</strong>; Theresienstadt
      to <strong>Auschwitz on 16 October 1944</strong>; and <strong>date of death 16 October 1944</strong>
      — the same day the second transport left.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      One discrepancy settles in the Bundesarchiv&rsquo;s favour. Yad Vashem gives <strong>Georg as born
      in 1884</strong>, agreeing with the Gedenkbuch and disagreeing with Arolsen&rsquo;s 1885. Two
      against one, and the two are the older and better-sourced. <strong>Lydia Leibholz</strong> appears
      on the <strong>Łódź ghetto list</strong>, which is where this archive already believed
      Georg&rsquo;s wife died on the same day he did.
    </p>
    <p class="verdict disp">
      <strong>A decoy, written down before it costs somebody a day.</strong> The same search returns
      <em>&ldquo;KURT LEIBHOLZ, 1927, Berlin, murdered&rdquo;</em>. <strong>That is not our Kurt.</strong>
      Ours is Kurt <em>Luwinski</em>, born 11 May 1912, who left Germany in 1933 and died in Johannesburg
      in 1968. A Kurt of the same city, fifteen years younger, murdered, is exactly the kind of
      near-match this archive has been caught by three times in a fortnight.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>A second archive, and it says the same thing</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      Everything on this page rested on one source: the Bundesarchiv&rsquo;s <em>Gedenkbuch</em>. On
      17 September 2026 the <strong>Arolsen Archives</strong> were searched for the first time — the
      international centre on Nazi persecution, thirty million documents naming some seventeen and a
      half million people. <strong>213 person entries carry the name Leibholz.</strong>
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Arolsen index entry</th><th>Born</th><th>Birthplace</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>LEIBHOLZ, JAKOB</strong></td><td>08.08.1884</td><td><strong>SCHUBIN</strong></td><td class="small">Exactly the Gedenkbuch, from records with no connection to it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LEIBHOLZ, JACOB</td><td>08.08.1884</td><td>—</td><td class="small muted">The same man again.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LEIBHOLZ, <em>Jacques Jaques</em></td><td>08.08.1884</td><td>—</td><td class="small">The same man <strong>French-spelled</strong> — which means a document made in French hands, a tracing or displaced-persons file rather than a German one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>LEIBHOLZ, OSKAR</strong></td><td>13.05.1887</td><td><strong>SCHUBIN</strong></td><td class="small">Exactly the Gedenkbuch. Four entries in all.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>LEIBHOLZ, ISRAEL OSKAR</strong></td><td>13.05.1887</td><td>—</td><td class="small"><strong>The compulsory name, on this family&rsquo;s own record.</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>LEIBHOLZ, GEORG ISRAEL</strong></td><td>07.06.1885</td><td>—</td><td class="small">The same, for Georg — and <strong>a year out</strong> from the Gedenkbuch&rsquo;s 1884. Same day, same month.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LEIBHOLZ, Siegfried</td><td>05.08.1925</td><td>—</td><td class="small">Kurt&rsquo;s younger brother, to the day.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Tracing case T/D 52.971</strong></td><td colspan="2">&ldquo;LEIBHOLZ, OSCAR born 13.05.1887&rdquo;</td><td class="small"><strong>Fourteen documents.</strong> After the war, somebody asked the International Tracing Service what had become of him.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <strong>The <em>Israel</em> is the thing to stop at.</strong> This site publishes the
      <a href={base + '/kurt/'}>1938 decree</a> that forced that name on German Jews. Here are two of
      these brothers carrying it. The law and the men are no longer on separate pages.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And there is a tracing case, which means somebody came looking.</strong> A T/D file is
      opened because a person asks. Fourteen documents were generated by that asking. Who asked is
      inside the file, and the likeliest askers are a brother who got out or a widow who did not.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>Samuel is the one absence, and it is recorded as unfinished rather than as a negative.</strong>
      A search under his name returns nothing, although both Yad Vashem and the Gedenkbuch have him.
      Only twenty-five of the 213 entries were read. He may be on a page not yet turned.
    </p>
    <p class="verdict disp">
      <strong>The documents were not opened, and that is deliberate.</strong> Reading one requires
      ticking Arolsen&rsquo;s declaration of consent to the use of sensitive personal data — a
      statement a named person makes about their own conduct. <strong>This archive does not tick it on
      anybody&rsquo;s behalf.</strong> Everything above is from the index, which asks nothing of anyone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>A surname leaving a city</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      The Zentral- und Landesbibliothek Berlin has digitised the <strong>Berliner Adressbücher</strong>
      — the city directory, <strong>one volume a year from 1799 to 1970</strong>, six to seven thousand
      pages each, listing householders by <strong>name, trade and street</strong>. It is free and
      full-text. This archive had never opened it until 17 September 2026, although the whole of this
      family lived in that city. <strong>1,047 pages carry the name Leibholz.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Counting them by year does something a list of four deportations cannot. <strong>These are page
      hits per annual volume, and the volumes are the same size throughout</strong> — six to seven and
      a half thousand pages, every year.
    </p>

    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Volume</th><th>Leibholz</th><th>Thimm</th><th>Hirschfeld</th><th>Bergmann</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1933</td><td><strong>24</strong></td><td>66</td><td>52</td><td>784</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1934</td><td>23</td><td>53</td><td>54</td><td>764</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1935</td><td>16</td><td>47</td><td>55</td><td>628</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1936</td><td>22</td><td>65</td><td>55</td><td>691</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1937</td><td>16</td><td>72</td><td>96</td><td>703</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1938</td><td>18</td><td>68</td><td>91</td><td>723</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1939</td><td>15</td><td>45</td><td>66</td><td>556</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1940</td><td>12</td><td>75</td><td>59</td><td>729</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1941</td><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>69</td><td>63</td><td>709</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1942</td><td>6</td><td>63</td><td>41</td><td>720</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1943</td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>75</strong></td><td>37</td><td>644</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <p>
      <strong>Leibholz goes from twenty-four to two. Thimm goes from sixty-six to seventy-five.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Thimm is the control, and it is not an idle one.</strong> Johanna Thimm of Sauerbaum is
      Kurt&rsquo;s own great-grandmother — East Prussian, and not Jewish. Her surname is as common in the
      last volume as in the first. So the scanner did not get worse, the Fraktur did not get harder, and
      the book did not get smaller. Bergmann, a large mixed name, is flat. Hirschfeld, plainly Jewish,
      declines but far less steeply, <strong>and this archive cannot explain that and does not pretend to</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      <strong>What the count cannot do, which matters more than the count.</strong> A page hit is a page
      and not a person; twenty-four is not twenty-four Leibholzes. The numbers are small, so a single
      year proves nothing and only the direction across four does. And above all, <strong>deportation,
      emigration and administrative exclusion from directories all look identical in a count</strong> —
      Jews were being struck out of German commercial listings from 1938 quite apart from being taken
      away. This table shows a name leaving a city. It does not say by which door, and nothing here
      should be read as though it did.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Still to find</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Whether Jacob has a <em>Stolperstein</em> in Schöneberg. The Leibholz stones located so far — Willmanndamm 6, Danckelmannstraße 44 — belong to other Leibholz families. <strong>Danckelmannstraße 44 is now identified</strong>: the 1939 census puts <strong>Hans Leibholz</strong>, born 25 January 1912 at <em>Preußisch Friedland, Kreis Schlochau</em>, his mother <strong>Else née Rothenberg</strong>, born at <em>Schlochau</em>, and his sister <strong>Liselott</strong> in that flat — <strong>the first Berlin Leibholz household documented as coming from Sally Leibholz&rsquo;s own district</strong>. A district is not a family, and it is not claimed as one.</li>
    <li>The Schubin (Szubin) Jewish registers, for Sally and Friederike's children and the brothers' births.</li>
    <li>The 17 May 1939 census card for Oskar at Berlin-Wilmersdorf, which recorded grandparents.</li>
    <li>Arolsen Archives, for all four, and for Elli and Werner.</li>
    <li><strong>Jacob in the Berlin directory, year by year, at Meraner Straße 2.</strong> The volumes are open and the counting works; reading a named line needs the page image, because the Fraktur columns jam exactly as the South African gazettes did. <strong>The year he stops appearing is the year the record of his life in that city stops.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>The eighteen Pages of Testimony under this surname.</strong> A Page names <em>who submitted it</em> — a living relative writing down a dead one. None has been opened.</li>
    <li><strong>The <em>Gedenkbuch Berlins</em> itself</strong>, Edition Hentrich 1995, which is where Transport 30 comes from and which this archive has been quoting at second hand without knowing it.</li>
    <li><strong>The other Berlin Leibholzes</strong> — Wilhelm 1890, Else 1889, Ruth 1923, Lieselotte 1923, Walter 1922, Chana 1938, Heinrich 1890, Albert 1886, Max 1877, Hans Israel 1912. Sally and Friederike had more sons than this archive has names for, and a Berlin Leibholz of the right age is where a fifth would show.</li>
    <li><strong>Auguste</strong> in the same books — before 1924 under <em>Luwinski</em>, after it under <em>Leibholz</em>, and after the war under <em>Fricke</em>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fifty in the memorial book, and only three born at Schubin</h2>
  <p>
    The Bundesarchiv Gedenkbuch was opened only to control a search for the eighth spelling of the
    name. The control returned <strong>fifty Leibholz</strong>, and the fifty say something the four
    this archive already held could not.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Three of fifty, and they are ours <span class="tag doc">New, 20 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Jacob, born 08.08.1884. Samuel, 28.06.1877. Oskar, 13.05.1887.</strong> No other
      Leibholz in the entire memorial book was born at Schubin. This family is not a fragment of a
      Posen population of the name — it is the only Leibholz household Schubin has in the record of
      the murdered.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the other forty-seven cluster somewhere else entirely: <strong>Glowitz 6 · Hammerstein 5 ·
      Karwen 5 · Schlochau 3 · Bärwalde 2 · Preußisch Friedland 2 · Tempelburg 2 · Danzig 2</strong>,
      with singles at Rummelsburg, Grabow, Zettin, Dammen, Loosen, Ostrowo, Bischofstein and
      Gelsenkirchen, and ten born in Berlin itself. Every major cluster is in Pomerania and West
      Prussia, within about sixty kilometres of Schlochau. <strong>The German centre of gravity of
      this surname is Kreis Schlochau, not Posen.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And Sally Leibholz — Jacob's father, Kurt's grandfather — was born at Hammerstein on
      7 July 1847 and married at Szubin in 1874.</strong> This archive has held both facts for days
      without drawing the line between them. The Schubin branch is a Hammerstein family that moved
      east, out of the surname's heartland rather than into it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Which thins the reasoning on the <a href={base + '/lines/'}>register of lines</a>. Georg's lane
      and the Kreis Schlochau lane have been kept apart from ours because they begin at Hammerstein
      and ours at Schubin — <em>but so did our own great-great-grandfather</em>. The lanes stay
      separate, because no document joins them. The reason for expecting them to be separate has gone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And Georg's wife was born eighteen kilometres from Auguste's village <span class="tag inf">A mechanism, not a proof</span></h3>
    <p>
      The book gives a birthplace this archive did not hold: <strong>Lydia Leibholz, born 16 January
      1885 at Bischofstein</strong> — Lydia née Perl, daughter of Julius and Mina, wife of Georg of
      the Michaelkirchstraße laundry, deported with him to Łódź and dead with him at Chełmno.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Bischofstein is in the Ermland, about eighteen kilometres from Sauerbaum</strong>, where
      Auguste Luwinski was born and where her mother's people farmed. And Georg Leibholz lived
      <a href={base + '/kurt/'}>next door to the Luwinskis</a> at Chodowieckistraße in 1913 and 1914.
    </p>
    <p>
      That proves nothing. Two Ermländer households next door to each other in Prenzlauer Berg is how
      chain migration works, and Berlin was full of them. <strong>But it is the first mechanism this
      archive has ever had</strong> for how a Catholic dressmaker from Kreis Rössel came to marry a
      Jewish merchant from Posen — through a neighbour who had made the same journey from the same
      place. The Kreis attribution for Bischofstein in 1885 has not been verified and should be
      before it is leaned on.
    </p>
  </div>


  <h2>The Weinstraße household was at Yad Vashem all along</h2>
  <p>
    This archive had the household from two sources and thought it had exhausted a third. The
    directories give <em>“Wilhelm, Vertreter, NO18 Weinstr 6”</em> from 1938, then in 1941
    <strong>“Wilhelm Israel, Bauarbeiter”</strong> — a sales representative reduced to a building
    labourer — and then nothing. The 1939 census gives Wilhelm Leibholz, born 30 December 1887, and
    Jenny née Rosenberg, born 11 February 1893, both <em>died before 8 May 1945</em>, which is what a
    census card and an absence can prove and no more.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the <a href={base + '/lines/'}>register of lines</a> carried a note saying he was
    <em>not</em> the Wilhelm Yad Vashem has. That was written off a search for LEIBHOLZ, and it was
    true of that search.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Both Wilhelms are there. One is filed under LEIPHOLZ <span class="tag doc">New, 21 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      Page of Testimony 5369884: <strong>“Leipholz · Willi · Willhelm · Date of Birth 30/12/1887 ·
      Married · Spouse Jenny · Spouse's Maiden Name Rosenberg · Address of Permanent Residence
      WEINSTR 6.”</strong> The birth date, the wife, her maiden name and the street. There is no room
      for a second reading.
    </p>
    <p>
      And it says the thing the census could not: <strong>place of death, Auschwitz.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Jenny's own page, 1122806, gives her birthplace as <strong>Schlochau</strong> and her parents as
      <strong>Isidor and Cecillie Rosenberg</strong> — two names this archive did not have. Schlochau
      is the district Sally Leibholz was born in, and the district
      <a href="#">the Gedenkbuch's fifty cluster in</a>. The Bundesarchiv gives her birthplace as
      Loosen instead; both are in the same corner of Pomerania, and this archive is not choosing
      between two sources on no evidence.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And somebody sat down and wrote it out <span class="tag inf">The only document here anyone chose to make</span></h3>
    <p>
      Both pages were filled in by the same man: <strong>Khanan Rosenberg Varadi</strong>, recorded as
      <em>Family</em> on Willi's and as <strong>Nephew</strong> on Jenny's. So a Rosenberg — a child of
      one of Jenny's brothers or sisters — who survived, and who at some point sat down and set out
      what had happened to his aunt and his uncle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything else in this archive is a clerk's form, a register line, a transport list, a card
      index. <strong>A Page of Testimony is the only kind of document here that somebody chose to
      make.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      It is not evidence of kinship to this family, and it is not treated as any. Nothing connects
      Willi Leipholz of Weinstraße 6 to the Schubin Leibholzes but a surname and a city, and the
      surname is the very thing these two days have shown to be unreliable in both directions.
      <strong>No approach has been made to the submitter or to anyone connected with him</strong>, and
      whether one ever should is not this archive's decision.
    </p>
  </div>


  <h2>The house he was moved into, and the man he was lodged with</h2>
  <p>
    Jacob's deportation line of 26 February 1943 gives his address as
    <strong>“Treuchtlingerstr. 4 b/Spier”</strong> — <em>bei Spier</em>, lodging with Spier. The
    archive read that correctly and could not say who Spier was.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Spier, H., Handelsvertreter <span class="tag doc">New, 21 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      The 1941 directory, street section, printed page 1615:
      <strong>“Rechte Seite · 2. 4 · E Eisenberg, G. Sara, Ww. · Bayer, W., Oberstleutin. ·
      Eberhardt, C., Kfm. · Jakob, W., Maurer · Stephan, H. Israel, Einricht. · Kochanowskea, F.,
      Hausgehilfin · Petras, M., kfm. Angest. · Rosenstock, L., kfm. Angest. · Spier, H.,
      Handelsvertr. · Boß, C. F., Kfm. · Wendorf, R., Abteilungsleit.”</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      There he is — a commercial agent, in the building two years before Jacob was put into it.
    </p>
    <p>
      And look at the owner. <strong>Eisenberg, G. <em>Sara</em>, Ww.</strong> — the widow who owned
      the house, carrying the name the 1938 decree forced on her. <strong>Stephan, H.
      <em>Israel</em></strong> on the floor below. A Jewish-owned house filling with Jewish tenants
      in 1940 is what a <em>Judenhaus</em> was, and the directory shows it happening without ever
      using the word.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And he is not in it yet <span class="tag inf">The move, bounded at both ends</span></h3>
    <p>
      No Leibholz anywhere in that 1941 entry — and the 1941 volume was compiled in the autumn of
      1940. So: <strong>Meraner Straße 2 on the census of 17 May 1939. Still not at Treuchtlinger in
      autumn 1940. At Treuchtlinger 4 <em>bei Spier</em> on 26 February 1943.</strong> The forced
      move happened inside that window.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 1942 and 1943 volumes would close it further, and the text layer will not give them up: a
      fuzzy search on the street returns only cross-references from neighbouring streets, and a
      combined search on the owner's and the lodger's names returns nothing in either volume. Those
      two pages have to be found by eye.
    </p>
  </div>

</Base>
